WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.212
IN is a membership operator.

00:00:02.212 --> 00:00:05.527
The inner query should return a one-column set.

00:00:05.527 --> 00:00:11.217
The outer value is compared with the values in that set.

00:00:11.217 --> 00:00:15.587
If it matches one of them, the membership test succeeds.

00:00:15.587 --> 00:00:18.722
Think of IN as asking, is this value in that returned list?

00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:25.927
IN answers a membership question: is this value present in the set returned by the subquery?

00:00:25.927 --> 00:00:31.027
Be especially careful with NOT IN when NULL can appear in that set,

00:00:31.027 --> 00:00:36.067
because three-valued logic can turn expected TRUE results into UNKNOWN.

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:40.693
For anti-matching, NOT EXISTS is often easier to reason about.
